            <div xml:id="d424">

                <pb facs="1219" n="1069" xml:id="pg1069"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 9757/4–5.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Chargé Brown to the
                            Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1907-11-29"><hi rendition="#italic">November 29,
                        1907</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 493.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge your
                    instruction No. 287 of the 13th instant (file No. 9757), in reference to the
                    opening by Turkish officials of the mail of American missionaries.</p>
                <p>I have already had occasion to unofficially call the attention of the Ottoman
                    postal authorities to abuses in this respect, and in compliance with your
                    instructions I have this day addressed a formal note to the minister of foreign
                    affairs (as per inclosure), reminding the Sublime Porte of its assurances in
                    1892, and the positive orders which it issued in the latter part of that year,
                    that letters addressed to Americans should not be detained.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Philip Brown.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d424-a1.xml" xpointer="d424-a1"/>
            </div>
